  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely correct one, with moderate confidence. I could not fully verify it by hand.\n\n- **Solution 1** gives $\\lfloor (3t-1)/2 \\rfloor$. Its sharpness claim rests on the cycle $C_{3t-1}$ with all capacities 1, but it never checks that other graphs cannot reach a larger diameter. Its own structural argument is only sketched.\n- **Solution 2** gives $2t-1$. Its counting argument is vague and its sharpness step is an unverified assertion, so I give it little weight.\n- **Solution 3** gives $3t-4$. The necessary condition $\\rho(G) \\le t-1$ is asserted and only spot-checked on small graphs. The pentagon-chain construction is concrete. For $t=3$, two $C_5$'s joined by an edge have diameter $2+1+2=5$, which is consistent with its formula. It agrees with my recollection of the published result for this problem, which I could not confirm with a search since I have no tools available here.\n\nSolution 1's bound is smaller than Solution 3's at $t=3$ (4 versus 5), so the two cannot both be sharp. Solution 3's construction gives a concrete graph of diameter $3t-4$, which makes its bound the better candidate.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
